Let a cluster graph of cluster depth 2 be a pair C = (G, T) where G = (V, E) is an undirected graph and T is a rooted tree of depth 2 whose leaves are exactly the vertices V of G, so that the internal (non-root) nodes of T define a partition of V into clusters. Suppose we are given two such cluster graphs C1 = (G1, T1) and C2 = (G2, T2) (modeling two biological networks that describe different functional aspects of the same underlying system), each equipped with a prescribed "conventional" planar layout π1 and π2 (a fixed drawing or mental map). Suppose further we are given a mapping Φ between the vertices and clusters of C1 and those of C2, which may be 1:1, 1:n, or n:m, and let F denote the set of edges induced by Φ between V(G1) and V(G2). Form the union graph G = G1 ∪ G2 ∪ (V(G1) ∪ V(G2), F).

Devise an algorithm that produces a layout π(G) of G satisfying the following requirements simultaneously:
(i) the layout reveals interactions between the two networks via the mapping edges F;
(ii) the layout displays the mapping Φ legibly, including its 1:n and n:m cases;
(iii) the cluster structures given by T1 and T2 are respected (vertices of the same cluster are drawn contiguously and clusters are visually identifiable);
(iv) the conventional layouts π1 of G1 and π2 of G2 are respected as subdrawings of π(G);
(v) disconnected components of one network are integrated into the layout of the other when this is appropriate given Φ;
(vi) the algorithm provides a tunable trade-off between preservation of the given layout/mental map of G1 and achieving an improved (e.g., aesthetically or readability-optimized) representation of G2.

Provide a formal specification, an algorithm realizing it, and quality measures under which the resulting layout can be evaluated.

